    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a loft addition for a residential build out in Oregon. I'm planning to use a Douglas Fir beam for the main span. The span is 14 feet, and I'm looking at a total dead load of about 15 psf plus a live load of 30 psf. Can you help me figure out what size beam I'll need to keep deflection within L/360 limits? I want to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy when people are up there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, deflection limits are crucial for comfort and safety. Douglas Fir beams have an allowable deflection of L/360, which means your beam can exceed the span length by 1/360th. Using the formula for maximum span deflection for beams and using the appropriate E (modulus of elasticity) and I (moment of inertia) values for Douglas Fir, you would likely end up needing a beam close to 12x24 in size. Be sure to double-check local codes and consider any additional factors that might come into play, such as the stiffness of the connecting trusses or rafters and potential span loading variations. It's also a good idea to speak with a structural engineer to confirm your design meets all prerequisites and safety measures.",
